This sounds really neat! I'm having trouble getting it to trigger, though. I've tried adding a `print_line` statement to this bit of code, so that I can see when it triggers and what values it creates for code completion options:
```cpp
const GDScriptParser::EnumNode *_enum = p_context.current_class->get_member(current_enum).m_enum;
for (int i = 0; i < _enum->values.size(); i++) {
    // TODO: Determine what case this covers.
	ScriptLanguage::CodeCompletionOption option(_enum->values[i].identifier->name, ScriptLanguage::CODE_COMPLETION_KIND_ENUM);
	print_line("Enum for specific context", option.display, "found");
	option.deprecated = _enum->doc_data.is_deprecated;
	r_result.insert(option.display, option);
}
```

Then, using this GDScript code:
```gdscript
enum SomeStuff { OPTION_ONE, OPTION_TWO }

func do_some_stuff(stuff: SomeStuff):
    print("do stuff", stuff)
    
func _ready():
    do_some_stuff(...
```

I don't seem to get any completion suggestions (and the `print_line` never prints anything):
![CleanShot 2024-12-12 at 10 00 04@2x](https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/1aef0cb8-44cf-4b24-a1e9-d0397c91aa8b)

If I type `SomeStuff.`, I do get suggestions at that point, but my `print_line` doesn't trigger (as I think makes sense).

Does this sound like it could be a bug, or did I misinterpret the conditions under which this should trigger?

